WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.139 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  I'm Keith Olbermann and this is the  resistance I do not know why Trump  planned to go to Puerto Rico I could not  fathom where he thought he was going to  find his sleazy victory lap photo op  except in a dry well-lit air-conditioned  hangar full of food and water miles from  any devastation but moreover I do not  know why he would go and pretend to care  it is now clear it is inarguable it is  inescapable that he hates the Puerto  Ricans  he spent the weekend making the plight  of the Americans devastated there about  him just as he spent the end of last  week making the plight of an American  community larger than 21 States not  about recovery but about making two  barely concealed threats that rebuilding  Puerto Rico will not take place until  Puerto Rico answers to the banks and  Wall Street as if he were not the  president of the United States but the  president of corporations or the  president of the economy or the  president of money to process that which  cannot be understood an American  president prioritizing himself and the  banks and Wall Street ahead of a dying  American community that's bigger than  Chicago or Philadelphia or Houston and  Dallas  that's nearly Houston and Dallas  combined to begin to conceive of the  mental illness that leads anybody to  think that way  one must examine one sad truth about  Donald Trump and when and where he came  from I was born and grew up in the  metropolitan New York City area 13 years  after Trump was born here and from the  people of his era from his places like  Queens or the Bronx and even the suburbs  the message to us children of the 60s  from him and the other children of the  30s and the 40s was direct and rarely  even hidden the New York City in which  they grew up had been ruined they  insisted by the Puerto Ricans in 1940  there were 61 thousand Puerto Ricans in  New York City by 1968 was 612 thousand  by 1970 it was eight hundred and  seventeen thousand look at the cultural  landmarks from that time the touching  1957 musical West Side Story  is the reimagining of Romeo and Juliet  only Romeo is a member of a white gang  in Manhattan and the men in Juliet's  life are all members of the Puerto Rican  gang the classic movie 12 Angry Men from  the same year carries both a plea  against prejudice against Puerto Ricans  in New York and some of the most  virulent examples of bigoted language  you will ever hear quote look you know  how these people lie says the character  played by Edie begley senior about  Puerto Ricans quote it's born in them  they don't know what the truth is and  let me tell you they don't need any real  big reason to kill someone either they  get drunk they're real big drinkers all  of them and bangs someone's lying in the  gutter nobody's blaming them for it  that's how they are by nature you know  what I mean  violent human life doesn't mean as much  to them as it does to us end quote when  I heard that film dialogue for the first  time as a child I flinched  because I heard language like that in  the suburbs of New York City and in New  York City itself in the 1970s and I  heard it from people adults who  considered themselves liberal and  without prejudice and I heard it in  public in places like Anki Stadium where  adults could hear it said and where to  my horror and shame nobody except my  parents acted like it was wrong except  those other adults didn't even call them  Puerto Ricans that hatred that very  specific very regional very intense  prejudice and bigotry and hatred is what  surrounded Donald Trump as a child  Donald Trump who had clearly already  begun life without human empathy whose  hatred and prejudice towards other  groups has been so evident with such  disastrous consequences with the wall  and the brutality of the ice raids and  charlottesville given his illnesses and  where and when he was raised his  attitude specifically towards Puerto  Rico and Puerto Ricans may be even worse  than all the others  it certainly dub tales with the 2008  interview with Howard Stern recovered  and posted last week by a man named Bill  Frisch lling at the site fact base it  was Trump telling the story of hosting a  $100,000 a table fundraiser the red  cross ball at mar-a-lago  Florida quoting what he told stern a man  about 80 years old very wealthy man a  lot of people didn't like him he fell  off the stage so what happens is this  guy falls off right on his face hits his  head and I thought he died and you know  what I did I said oh my god that's  disgusting and I turned away I couldn't  you know he was right in front of me and  I turned away I didn't want to touch him  he's bleeding all over the place I felt  terrible you know beautiful marble floor  didn't look so good  it changed color became very red I was  saying get that blood cleaned up it's  disgusting  the next day I forgot to call the man to  see he's okay  it's just not my thing and that man  wasn't Puerto Rican Sunday John  Dickerson of CBS asked Speaker Paul Ryan  about Trump and race I do really believe  Ryan said his hearts in the right place  there is no heart  thus for Puerto Rico there is no hope  resist remove peace my book based on  more than 100 of these commentaries will  be available shortly there will also be  an audio version it will be about 20  hours long

